{% extends 'AdminInterfaceBundle:Layout:list.html.twig' %}
{% block listpane_content %}
	<section class="dp-paneblock dp-listpane">
		<header>
			<h3>Urgencies</h3>
		</header>
		<article>
			<ul class="dp-item-list">
				<li ng-repeat="urgency in TicketUrgenciesList.urgencies">
					<a>
						<div class="pull-right">
							<span class="badge" style="margin-top: -2px;" tooltip="The number of tickets with this urgency" tooltip-placement="left">Tickets: {{ ng_var('urgency.ticket_count') }}</span>
						</div>
						<span class="urgency-tag urgency-{{ ng_var('urgency.num') }}">
							Urgency {{ ng_var('urgency.num') }}
						</span>
					</a>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</article>
	</section>
{% endblock %}
{% block listpane_help %}
	<header>
		<h1>A ticket's Urgency score indicates how quickly your agents should resolve it.</h1>

		<p>Urgency scores range from 1 to 10, where 10 is the most urgent. Urgency can be set by agents, or you can set it automatically.
		</p>

	</header>
	<article>
		<section>
			<p>Urgency is designed to help your agents decide which tickets to attend to first. Urgency scores for each ticket are displayed prominently in the agent interface, with the same color-coding you see here. Agents can also sort their tickets by urgency.</p>
			<p>You can set, increase or decrease urgency with:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>escalations</strong></li>
				<li><strong>triggers</strong></li>
				<li><strong>SLA</strong> states (warning or failure)</li>
				<li><strong>macros</strong></li>
			</ul>


		</section>
		<section>
		<h2><span>FAQ</span></h2>
		<dl>
			<dt><em>Q:</em>What's the difference between urgency and priority?</dt>
			<dd><em>A:</em>
				Urgency is a number from 1 to 10. Priorities are an ordered set of named priority levels (for example "Routine", "Urgent", "Critical"; you define the priority levels). Users can set and view priority from the portal, whereas urgency is for agent reference only. Trigger actions can be used to increase or decrease urgency by a relative amount (e.g. increase urgency by 5, reduce urgency by 2), whereas trigger actions can only set a particular priority.
			</dd>


			<dt><em>Q:</em>What happens if urgency goes below 1 or above 10?</dt>
					<dd><em>A:</em>
					If a trigger action or other automatic process would set a ticket's urgency below 1, the urgency is set to 1. If the urgency would go above 10, it is set to 10.
				</dd>

		</dl>
</section>
		<section class="related-content">
			<h2><span>Related Content</span></h2>
			<ul>
				<li><i class="fa fa-book"></i><a href="https://support.deskpro.com/guides/topic/241#urgency" target="_blank">Urgency</a>
				<li><i class="fa fa-book"></i><a href="https://support.deskpro.com/guides/topic/381" target="_blank">Setting urgency automatically</a></li>
				<li><i class="fas fa-file-alt"></i><a href="https://support.deskpro.com/kb/articles/289" target="_blank">Triggers example: tickets from organization managers are more urgent</a></li>


			</ul>
	</section>
	</article>
{% endblock %}
